<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>COPD in an 82-year-old former smoker, now confirmed on spirometry as GOLD 3. Please confirm the grade and group, review the inhaler choice and the place of an inhaled corticosteroid, assess his oxygen needs, and advise on pulmonary rehabilitation and lung cancer screening. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree with GOLD 3, group B, and is anything other than COPD contributing to his breathlessness?</item><item>Is a long-acting bronchodilator pair in a soft mist inhaler the right start, and is there any place for an inhaled corticosteroid?</item><item>Does he need oxygen at rest or on exertion?</item><item>Does the alpha-1 antitrypsin level need further testing, and does he need chest CT for emphysema, lung volume reduction, or lung cancer screening?</item><item>Should he start pulmonary rehabilitation now, and what should we watch for?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Nimoy is seen at the request of Dr. Benedetti four weeks after starting tiotropium-olodaterol. He smoked heavily for many years, quit about 30 years ago through a cessation program, and has been limited by breathlessness for some time. He now stops after about 100 meters on level ground. His mornings are a little easier on the new inhaler, and his albuterol use has fallen to about four times a week. He has little cough, a small amount of clear morning sputum, and no wheeze at rest, chest pain, orthopnea, ankle swelling, snoring, or reflux. He has had no exacerbation in the past year. He is keen to keep active and to warn others about smoking. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Leonard Nimoy was born in Boston on March 26, 1931. [D]</item><item>He played Mr. Spock in the Star Trek television series from 1966 to 1969, later acted in and directed Star Trek films, and directed other films, including the comedy Three Men and a Baby. [D]</item><item>In a Twitter post at the end of January 2014 he announced that he had chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: &quot;I quit smoking 30 yrs ago. Not soon enough. I have COPD. Grandpa says, quit now!! LLAP&quot;. [D]</item><item>In early February 2014 he posted &quot;I&apos;m doing OK. Just can&apos;t walk distances,&quot; and ABC News reported that he had been seen at an airport in a wheelchair. [D]</item><item>In February 2014 he urged smokers to quit: &quot;Smokers, please understand. If you quit after you&apos;re diagnosed with lung damage it&apos;s too late.&quot; [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

