    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>COPD, GOLD 3, group B (J44.9). Confirmed: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC 0.43 with FEV1 46 percent predicted is GOLD grade 3. CAT 19 today (22 at intake), mMRC 3, and no exacerbation in 12 months place him in group B. Normal hemoglobin, a normal ECG, and no signs of heart failure make another main cause of his breathlessness unlikely. [S]</item><item>Oxygen assessment. Resting saturation 93 percent. On the 6-minute walk he covered 270 m with a lowest saturation of 89 percent. He meets no criterion for long-term oxygen, and the evidence does not support oxygen for moderate exertional desaturation; an arterial blood gas is not needed while resting saturation stays above 92 percent. [S]</item><item>Alpha-1 antitrypsin and imaging. 138 mg/dL is normal, which excludes severe deficiency; no phenotyping is needed. He is not eligible for lung cancer screening, because he quit more than 15 years ago. Chest CT is not needed now: there is no suspected complication, and lung volume reduction would only be considered if he stayed severely limited after optimal treatment and rehabilitation. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Intake spirometry before and after bronchodilator, the blood eosinophil count, the alpha-1 antitrypsin level, hemoglobin, creatinine, the intake ECG, the CAT and mMRC scores, and today&apos;s 6-minute walk test with continuous oximetry. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Continue tiotropium-olodaterol two inhalations each morning, with albuterol as needed; the soft mist inhaler suits him because it needs no strong inspiratory effort. [S]</item><item>No inhaled corticosteroid: he has had no exacerbation and his eosinophils are below 300 per microliter. Reconsider after two moderate exacerbations or one hospital stay in a year if eosinophils are 100 or more. [S]</item><item>Pulmonary rehabilitation now, with a 6-minute walk at entry (done today) and at exit. [S]</item><item>No oxygen; check saturation at every visit and reassess if resting saturation falls to 92 percent or less. [S]</item><item>No further alpha-1 antitrypsin testing and no chest CT at present. [S]</item><item>PCV20 now, RSV vaccine before the next respiratory virus season, and yearly high-dose influenza and COVID-19 vaccines. [S]</item><item>Written action plan for flare-ups; spirometry yearly. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Benedetti the same day; the patient agrees with the plan. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care will continue monitoring on the CMID schedule. Pulmonology will see him again after any hospital stay for an exacerbation, two or more moderate exacerbations in a year, a fall in resting saturation to 92 percent or less, or loss of the benefit from rehabilitation. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
